<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Genoese Amasra</h1><p class="st-meta">1261 CE – 1460 CE (approx.) · capital Amasra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Genoese trading colony on the Black Sea coast of Anatolia, protected by walls that still carry Genoese coats of arms.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nymphaeum-1261">Treaty of Nymphaeum</a> of 1261 opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a>, Genoese merchants established themselves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasra">Amasra</a> (Samastri), an old Byzantine port on a pair of headlands, which became one of their stations on the coast between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/genoese-gazaria">Caffa</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasra">Amasra</a> was governed by Genoese consuls subordinate to the colonial authorities at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galata">Pera</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/genoese-gazaria">Caffa</a>. Its population was largely Greek, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> merchants and soldiers. The town traded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, grain and slaves and served as a harbour of refuge. The Genoese strengthened its castle, and their inscriptions and arms survive in the walls.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1460 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> appeared before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasra">Amasra</a> and the Genoese surrendered without a fight. Much of the population was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1261 CE – 1460 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.80%;width:3.96%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1261 CE – 1460 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Amasra</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Latin Christianity, Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ligurian, Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Samastri</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1360&amp;focus=genoese-amasra" aria-label="Genoese Amasra on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5468 691 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Genoese Amasra lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5788 947L5214 863M5788 947L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5214 863h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Amasra. <a href="/atlas?year=1360&amp;focus=genoese-amasra">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

